description: Performance, reliability, production-readiness, and reusable UI/data patterns
alwaysApply: true
---

# Performance and Operations

- Optimize Core Web Vitals by default:
  - keep components server-rendered when possible,
  - stream slow sections with Suspense,
  - minimize client JavaScript.
- Choose rendering strategy explicitly (dynamic, ISR, static, or PPR where available) based on data volatility and UX needs.
- Use `next/image` for content images; set explicit dimensions and prioritize only true LCP assets.
- Implement file-based loading/error boundaries (`loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`) for route segments with remote data dependencies.
- In Server Actions and handlers, return safe user-facing errors and structured logs for debugging.
- Validate required environment variables at runtime; fail fast instead of allowing silent misconfiguration.
- Prefer structured server-side logging and error reporting (for example Sentry/Axiom patterns already used in the codebase).
- For major UI/data changes, include a measurement plan (Lighthouse and/or bundle analysis).

# Reusability and composition

- Prefer reusable components and shared helpers over copy-paste: parameterize with props, `children`, and composition (slots/wrappers) instead of near-duplicate files.
- Before adding a new component, search the codebase for existing primitives, layouts, and patterns (`src/components/ui`, shared store chrome, `cn()`, tokens) and extend them when they fit.
- Extract repeated UI or logic into small, focused units with clear props and stable boundaries; avoid mega-components that mix unrelated concerns.
- Place shared UI in obvious shared locations; keep route- or feature-specific pieces colocated until a second real use case justifies promotion.
- Reuse data-access and formatting utilities across routes and actions; centralize constants and Zod schemas at trust boundaries rather than redefining shapes inline.
- Balance DRY with clarity: do not abstract one-off or unstable patterns prematurely; refactor to reuse once the shape of the API is proven in two or more call sites.
